  ("1.6. CHAPTER REMARKS" 0.1924 0.4283 0.1063 0.1193 0.1193 0.0130 0.01447 0.2739 0.0284 "KMLPFE+CMSL10" 0.0130 nil t 2)
  ("17" 0.7934 0.8117 0.1076 0.1194 0.1194 0.0117 nil nil 0.0182 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 0)
  ("In summary, qsort has sorted the example list into numerical order. More" 0.1924 0.8117 0.1441 0.1558 0.1558 0.0117 0.00819 0.2920 0.0169 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 11)
  ("generally, this function produces a sorted version of any list of numbers. The" 0.1924 0.8122 0.1602 0.1719 0.1719 0.0117 0.0063 0.2501 0.0751 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 12)
  ("first equation for qsort states that the empty list is already sorted, while the" 0.1924 0.8118 0.1763 0.1880 0.1880 0.0117 0.00657 0.2650 0.0319 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 13)
  ("second states that any non-empty list can be sorted by inserting the first" 0.1924 0.8121 0.1924 0.2041 0.2041 0.0117 0.00898 0.2266 0.0530 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 12)
  ("number between the two lists that result from sorting the remaining numbers" 0.1924 0.8122 0.2085 0.2203 0.2203 0.0117 0.00588 0.2983 0.0609 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 11)
  ("that are smaller and larger than this number. This method of sorting is called" 0.1924 0.8115 0.2246 0.2364 0.2364 0.0117 0.00509 0.3078 0.0336 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 13)
  ("quicksort, and is one of the best such methods known." 0.1924 0.6287 0.2407 0.2525 0.2525 0.0117 0.00615 0.2474 0.0783 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 9)
  ("The above implementation of quicksort is an excellent example of the power" 0.2208 0.8117 0.2567 0.2684 0.2684 0.0117 0.00434 0.3352 0.0317 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 11)
  ("of Haskell, being both clear and concise. Moreover, the function qsort is also" 0.1924 0.8117 0.2728 0.2845 0.2845 0.0117 0.0065 0.2621 0.0147 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 12)
  ("more general than might be expected, being applicable not just with numbers," 0.1924 0.8122 0.2889 0.3006 0.3006 0.0117 0.00516 0.3045 0.0398 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 11)
  ("but with any type of ordered values. More precisely, the type" 0.1924 0.6855 0.3050 0.3167 0.3167 0.0117 0.00617 0.2668 0.0277 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 10)
  ("qsort :: Ord a ⇒[a ] →[a ]" 0.3919 0.6041 0.3375 0.3505 0.3505 0.0117 0.00499 0.6126 0.0390 "KMLPFO+CMTI10" 0.0130 nil t 7)
  ("states that for any type a of ordered values, qsort is a function that maps" 0.1924 0.8120 0.3657 0.3775 0.3775 0.0117 0.00798 0.1999 0.0457 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 14)
  ("between lists of such values. Haskell supports many different types of ordered" 0.1924 0.8119 0.3818 0.3936 0.3936 0.0117 0.00588 0.2631 0.0644 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 11)
  ("values, including numbers, single characters such as ’a’, and strings of char-" 0.1924 0.8117 0.3979 0.4100 0.4100 0.0117 0.0064 0.2398 0.0535 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 11)
  ("acters such as \"abcde\". Hence, for example, the function qsort could also be" 0.1924 0.8121 0.4140 0.4261 0.4261 0.0117 0.00651 0.2390 0.0467 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 12)
  ("used to sort a list of characters, or a list of strings." 0.1924 0.6004 0.4301 0.4419 0.4419 0.0117 0.00618 0.2016 0.0358 "KMLOPE+CMR10" 0.0130 nil nil 11)
